delicious is not a word we normally use  to describe hospital foods but one chef  is changing perceptions of what  cafeteria food can be with the help of  some international cuisine chef Kobe  Smith revamped the menu at Arkansas  Heart Hospital and his signature disc  dish is authentic ramen Adriana Diaz  visited the Little Rock Hospital to see  how the menu is attracting more than  just patients and employees we actually  work on the campus of Arkansas  Children's Hospital so you came from  another hospital to this hospital to  have lunch since we've been doing this  we've had thousands and thousands of  people come to this hospital for one  reason only and that's just to eat our  food three days a week  chef Coby Smith dishes out what's  arguably the most authentic Japanese  ramen in town so do you always come when  they're serving ramen Monday Wednesday  Friday just now all three days yes ma'am  sometimes on Fridays I'll get two  Ramin in Little Rock Raman in Little  Rock at a hospital it's right are y'all  doing up here for a few years chef Coby  has been turning around the hospital  kitchen good  after dr. Bruce Murphy the hospital CEO  said the food was too institutional food  that has been prepared elsewhere canned  and you open a bag and you served the  people after you warmed it up not fresh  food not vibrant food hospital food it's  hospital food and we wanted to change  that he demanded that ingredients be  crushed the food delicious oh and one  more thing he wanted to have authentic  Japanese ramen I was being a little bit  selfish because I love ramen noodles and  I figured everybody else would love  ramen he discovered his love for the  dish while traveling in Asia I didn't  think twice about where the best place  in the world was for him to learn how to  cook ramen is Tokyo we got to go to  Tokyo so for a week chef Coby visited  the doctor's favorite ramen shops and  met with their chefs taking notes and  videos along the way these guys do the  same job every day over and over again  for 30 40 50 years they take their craft  so literally and there so it's an honor  for them once back in Arkansas he spent  eight months trying to turn his research  into ramen starting with the  all-important broth it's like a small  child you have to nurture it and watch  it and make sure all the ingredients are  the same can you even learn how to make  authentic ramen in one week no you can't  do it but you do the best you can with  what you have we're in the South so you  know we have a southern twist to a lot  of our ramen dishes twists like crawfish  and okra but today was pork belly day I  was very skeptical little rock food  critic Kevin shaylen resisted the  concept of hospital made ramen until he  tried it if a restaurant was serving  this kind of ramen would you rate it as  highly yeah you know just because it's  served in a hospital I don't rate it any  differently  it is good ramen so good that on ramen  days parking becomes a problem it's been  so successful that I can't get to my my  ramen shop because there are other  people in line that's a rare problem in  the realm of hospital food for CBS this  morning Adriana Diaz Little Rock
 

